{"answer":"The claimed conclusion that √2 is rational is false. The argument is a valid proof by contradiction once its implicit parity inference is justified: from a² even one must prove that a is even. It then forces both a and b to be even, contradicting that a/b was in lowest terms. Thus √2 is irrational.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume rationality, select a lowest-terms integer fraction, and derive a common factor 2.","id":"direct-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The parity inference is supplied explicitly, so the asserted contradiction is justified."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary assumption for reductio: √2 is rational.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"id":"claim-rational-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-lowest-terms","step-square","step-a-square-even","step-a-even","step-b-square-even","step-b-even","step-contradiction"],"statement":"Assume, for contradiction, that √2 is rational.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Lowest-terms representation theorem: every rational number has a representation a/b with integers a,b, b ≠ 0, and gcd(a,b)=1. Applied to the rationality assumption in step-lowest-terms.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-lowest-terms","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There are integers a,b with b ≠ 0, √2 = a/b, and gcd(a,b)=1.","step_ids":["step-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in the displayed arithmetic and parity steps.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-lowest-terms"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-both-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Both a and b are even.","step_ids":["step-a-even","step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction discharges the temporary rationality assumption in step-contradiction.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption","claim-lowest-terms","claim-both-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"√2 is irrational.","step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-lowest-terms","justification":"Apply the lowest-terms representation theorem to the temporary rationality assumption.","statement":"Choose integers a,b with b ≠ 0, √2 = a/b, and gcd(a,b)=1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-lowest-terms"],"id":"step-square","justification":"Squaring √2 = a/b and multiplying by b² gives the equality.","statement":"a² = 2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square"],"id":"step-a-square-even","justification":"The right-hand side 2b² is divisible by 2.","statement":"a² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-a-square-even"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"If a were odd, a=2k+1 for some integer k, so a²=4k²+4k+1=2(2k²+2k)+1 would be odd, contradicting step-a-square-even.","statement":"a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square","step-a-even"],"id":"step-b-square-even","justification":"Write a=2m using step-a-even. Then 4m²=2b² by step-square, hence b²=2m².","statement":"b² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-b-square-even"],"id":"step-b-even","justification":"The same odd-square calculation used in step-a-even shows that an integer with even square is even.","statement":"b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-lowest-terms","step-a-even","step-b-even"],"id":"step-contradiction","justification":"Steps step-a-even and step-b-even say that 2 divides both a and b, contrary to gcd(a,b)=1 from step-lowest-terms. This contradiction discharges the assumption that √2 is rational.","statement":"The temporary assumption that √2 is rational is false."}],